.TH MKFS.SIEFS 8
.SH NAME
mkfs.siefs \- make a SieFS file system in an image file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B build/host/mkfs.siefs
.RB [ \-s
.IR size ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR label ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR uid : gid ]
.I image
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkfs.siefs
runs on the build machine (Linux). It writes a new, empty SieFS file
system into
.IR image ,
optionally filled with a copy of a directory, and prints its size,
objects and free blocks.
.B make
uses it for
.B build/disk.img
(from
.BR build/rootfs )
and the tests' fresh disks.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-s " size"
Create
.I image
with this size
.RB ( 64M ,
.BR 3G ...;
sparse); without
.B \-s
an existing image keeps its size.
.TP
.BI \-L " label"
The volume's label (default
.BR SIEOS ).
.TP
.BI \-d " dir"
Copy
.IR dir 's
contents (files, directories, symbolic links, with their modes) into the
new file system.
.TP
.BI \-o " uid" : gid
The copied files' owner (default 0:0, root).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0; 1 if something could not be made or copied; 2 for a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
build/host/mkfs.siefs \-s 64M \-L SIEOS \-d build/rootfs test.img
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR siefs (8),
.BR fsck.siefs (8),
.BR siefs (5)
